Do not tear down clocks and rails when runtime suspend failed

rknpu_power_off() discards what pm_runtime_put_sync() returns, then
unconditionally disables the clock bulk and both regulators and reports
success. Before this series the suspend callback could not really fail,
so that was academic. Patch 0008 gives it two failure modes it can
report - it takes clock references and restores the pending SCMI rate -
and a failure there leaves the PM core treating the device as active.
Cutting its clocks and rail out from under it invites exactly the
unpowered-register access the rest of this series exists to avoid, and
returning 0 tells rknpu_power_put() to leave power_refcount at zero
while the hardware is still on.

Check it and bail out with the error, matching what the iommu path
already does a few lines further down. The reference has to be taken
back by hand: pm_runtime_put_sync() decrements whether or not the
callback succeeds, so without that a retry underflows the usage count.

Also scope the `val` declaration to the branch that still uses it.
Patch 0007 compiled out the readx_poll_timeout() that read it, leaving
an unused variable on mainline builds.

Signed-off-by: Isaiah DeRose-Wilson <isaiahdw@gmail.com>

--- a/rknpu_drv.c
+++ b/rknpu_drv.c
@@ -1025,12 +1025,53 @@
 
 #ifndef FPGA_PLATFORM
 	int ret;
+#if KERNEL_VERSION(6, 0, 0) > LINUX_VERSION_CODE
 	bool val;
+#endif
 
 	rknpu_devfreq_lock(rknpu_dev);
 #endif
 
+#ifndef FPGA_PLATFORM
+	/*
+	 * Shut the SCMI gate first. Doing it after the suspended check below
+	 * leaves a window where a forced resume - power/control=on - runs the
+	 * resume callback with hw_powered still true, and it programs a rate
+	 * while the teardown underneath it takes the clocks and domains away.
+	 * Every path out of here that leaves the hardware up puts it back.
+	 */
+	WRITE_ONCE(rknpu_dev->hw_powered, false);
+
+	ret = pm_runtime_put_sync(dev);
+	if (ret < 0) {
+		/*
+		 * The reference is dropped whether or not the suspend
+		 * callback succeeded, so take it back: the caller restores
+		 * power_refcount and will come through here again.
+		 */
+		WRITE_ONCE(rknpu_dev->hw_powered, true);
+		pm_runtime_get_noresume(dev);
+		LOG_DEV_ERROR(dev, "failed to suspend npu: %d\n", ret);
+		rknpu_devfreq_unlock(rknpu_dev);
+		return ret;
+	}
+	/*
+	 * Zero only means no error. __pm_runtime_idle() returns 0 without
+	 * suspending anything when the usage count did not reach zero, which
+	 * is what happens while npu_devfreq_target() holds its reference
+	 * across a rate change. Continuing here would drop the domains and
+	 * clocks underneath an in-flight SCMI transition. Hand the reference
+	 * back and let the caller retry once devfreq is done.
+	 */
+	if (!pm_runtime_status_suspended(dev)) {
+		WRITE_ONCE(rknpu_dev->hw_powered, true);
+		pm_runtime_get_noresume(dev);
+		rknpu_devfreq_unlock(rknpu_dev);
+		return -EBUSY;
+	}
+#else
 	pm_runtime_put_sync(dev);
+#endif
 
 	if (rknpu_dev->multiple_domains) {
 #ifndef FPGA_PLATFORM
